<p><strong>Adult dogs have 42 teeth. Adult cats have 30.</strong> Puppies start out with 28 baby teeth and kittens with 26, and both swap them for a permanent set by around six or seven months of age.</p>

<p>If you only wanted the number, perhaps to settle an argument, that's it. The American Kennel Club and VCA Animal Hospitals both give these figures, and breed doesn't change them. A Chihuahua and a Great Dane are both supposed to have 42 teeth, packed into very different jaws. The rest of this post deals with the more practical question of whether your own pet has the right teeth in the right places, and what it means if they don't.</p>

<h2>Dog and Cat Teeth at a Glance</h2>

<p>Here are the counts and the rough timeline side by side. The ages are approximate. Individual animals vary a little, and dogs vary a little by breed.</p>

<table>
<thead>
<tr><th></th><th>Dogs</th><th>Cats</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Baby (deciduous) teeth</td><td>28</td><td>26</td></tr>
<tr><td>Adult (permanent) teeth</td><td>42</td><td>30</td></tr>
<tr><td>Baby teeth start coming in</td><td>About 3 weeks (sometimes as early as 2)</td><td>About 3 weeks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Full set of baby teeth</td><td>About 6–8 weeks</td><td>About 6–8 weeks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Baby teeth start falling out</td><td>About 3½–5 months</td><td>From about 10 weeks to 6 months</td></tr>
<tr><td>Full adult set in place</td><td>Around 6 months (back molars can run to 7 or so)</td><td>By about 6–7 months</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Where 42 Teeth Actually Go</h2>

<p>The AKC breaks a dog's adult mouth into four kinds of teeth. There are 12 incisors, the small front teeth, six on top and six on the bottom. There are 4 canines, the long "fangs," one at each corner. Behind those come 16 premolars, eight upper and eight lower, which do most of the shearing. At the back are 10 molars, the flat grinding teeth, and these are split unevenly: four on top and six on the bottom. So the lower jaw holds 22 teeth and the upper jaw 20. That's ten more than an adult human has.</p>

<p>Cats keep the same front end: 12 incisors and 4 canines. Further back they have far fewer teeth, with just 10 premolars (six upper, four lower) and 4 molars (one in each quarter of the mouth). That adds up to 30.</p>

<p>Puppies and kittens have no baby molars at all. The 28 puppy teeth and 26 kitten teeth are all incisors, canines and premolars. The molars only arrive with the permanent set, which is why the adult count is so much higher.</p>

<h2>The Teething Timeline</h2>

<h3>Puppies</h3>

<p>Puppies are born without teeth. According to PetMD, baby teeth start breaking through at around three weeks (an AKC-quoted vet puts it as early as two), starting with the incisors, then the canines, then the premolars. By six to eight weeks a puppy usually has all 28.</p>

<p>The changeover starts at around three and a half to five months, with the incisors going first. Canines tend to follow at five to six months, and the adult premolars and molars fill in over the next couple of months. PetMD puts most puppies at a full adult set by about six months, and the AKC's timeline lets the back teeth run to around eight months in some dogs. Expect extra chewing and mouthing through this stretch. It's also normal never to find a single baby tooth, because puppies often swallow them while eating, and PetMD notes this is generally harmless.</p>

<h3>Kittens</h3>

<p>Kittens follow nearly the same schedule. VCA says baby teeth begin erupting around three weeks and are usually all in by six to eight weeks. Teething runs from about ten weeks to six months as the permanent incisors push out the baby ones, and most kittens have all 30 adult teeth by six or seven months.</p>

<p>Kitten teething is quieter than puppy teething, but you may notice some drooling, a few days of picky eating, a bit of irritability, and what VCA calls "kitten breath," a distinctive odor that lasts as long as teething does. VCA suggests starting to brush a kitten's teeth as soon as you bring them home, then taking a short break while the teeth are actually changing over. Our <a href="/tools/cat-dental-care-guide/">cat dental care guide</a> covers how to build that habit without a fight.</p>

<h2>"How Many Teeth Should My Pet Have?" Is a Different Question</h2>

<p>Counting is the trivia part. In practice, almost nobody is going to count 42 teeth in a wriggling Labrador, and you don't need to. What matters for health is whether teeth are where they should be: no extras, no unexplained gaps, and enough room for the ones that are there. The AVMA recommends a veterinary dental check at least once a year, and it specifically lists "extra teeth or retained baby teeth" as a reason to go in sooner.</p>

<h3>One too many: a baby tooth that didn't fall out</h3>

<p>The most common counting problem in young pets is a retained (or "persistent") baby tooth. The adult tooth comes in, but the baby tooth beside it never falls out, so you see two teeth crowded into one spot. VCA says the upper canines are the usual culprits, followed by the lower canines and then the incisors. This isn't just cosmetic. The doubled-up teeth trap food, which leads to tartar, gum inflammation and eventually periodontal disease. The baby tooth can also push the adult tooth out of position. A lower canine forced inward can end up jabbing the roof of the mouth, which is painful.</p>

<p>VCA's rule is blunt: "No two teeth should be in the same socket at the same time." The AKC says the same thing for puppies: any baby teeth that don't fall out need to be extracted by a veterinarian. That requires anesthesia and dental X-rays. Owners often ask whether it can wait for the spay or neuter appointment, since that also involves anesthesia. VCA's guidance for kittens is not to wait, because early extraction usually lets the adult tooth move into its proper place. For a puppy, ask your vet about timing rather than assuming it can be bundled in. Our <a href="/tools/dog-spay-neuter-guide/">dog spay and neuter guide</a> covers the timing questions around that procedure. VCA also recommends checking a kitten's mouth weekly until about seven or eight months old, and the same habit works well for puppies.</p>

<h3>One too few: a gap where a tooth should be</h3>

<p>A gap means something different depending on age. In a young animal, a missing tooth may never have developed, or it may be stuck under the gum and never have erupted. You can't tell which by looking. Dental X-rays are how a vet sees what's happening below the gumline, and the AVMA notes that most dental disease sits below the gumline where owners can't see it.</p>

<p>In an adult, a missing tooth is more often one that has been lost. VCA estimates that more than two-thirds of dogs over three have some degree of periodontal disease, and left untreated it loosens teeth until they fall out. Fractured teeth are the other common problem in dogs. In cats, the main cause is tooth resorption. Cornell's Feline Health Center calls it the most common cause of tooth loss in cats and says 30 to 70 percent of cats show some sign of it. It often first appears as a pinkish spot where the tooth meets the gum, and it can be quite painful. So an older cat with fewer than 30 teeth isn't unusual, but it's worth knowing why.</p>

<h3>The right number, not enough room</h3>

<p>Small dogs don't get fewer teeth to match their smaller jaws. A Pug, a Yorkie or a French Bulldog is meant to fit the same 42 teeth as a German Shepherd. VCA notes that small breeds and brachycephalic (short-nosed) dogs are more prone to periodontal disease precisely because of their small jaws and crowded teeth. Overlapping or rotated teeth leave more places for plaque to settle, so daily brushing matters more for these dogs, not less. Our <a href="/tools/dog-teeth-brushing-guide/">dog teeth brushing guide</a> walks through how to do it. Cornell lists tooth crowding as a contributor to plaque buildup in cats as well.</p>

<h2>When a Tooth Problem Is Worth a Vet Visit</h2>

<p>None of these is usually an emergency, but each is worth booking an appointment for rather than waiting for the next annual checkup:</p>

<ul>
<li>A baby tooth still sitting next to its adult replacement, especially past about six or seven months.</li>
<li>A lower canine that seems to angle inward or touch the roof of the mouth.</li>
<li>A new gap in an adult mouth, or any tooth that's loose, broken or discolored.</li>
<li>A pinkish spot or "hole" at the gumline in a cat.</li>
<li>Drooling, dropping food, chewing on one side, or pawing at the mouth.</li>
<li>Bad breath that doesn't go away, bleeding gums, or swelling around the face or jaw.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Quick Answers to the Other Ways People Ask This</h2>

<h3>How many teeth does an adult dog have?</h3>
<p>42: 20 in the upper jaw and 22 in the lower. Puppies have 28 baby teeth, which are replaced by around six months of age.</p>

<h3>How many teeth should a cat have?</h3>
<p>An adult cat should have 30 teeth, and a kitten 26 baby teeth. Many adult cats end up with fewer because of tooth resorption or extractions, so a lower count isn't unusual. It's still worth asking your vet about if you don't know the history.</p>

<h3>Do all dogs have the same number of teeth?</h3>
<p>They're all meant to. Breed and size don't change the count. A toy breed has the same 42 teeth as a giant breed, just more tightly packed. An individual dog can be missing a tooth that never developed, but that's a one-off, not a breed standard.</p>

<h3>Do dogs have more teeth than people?</h3>
<p>Yes. Adult humans have 32 teeth, so an adult dog has ten more. Cats have two fewer than us.</p>

<h3>Is it normal if I never find any of my puppy's or kitten's baby teeth?</h3>
<p>Completely. Both puppies and kittens often swallow baby teeth while eating, and it's generally harmless.</p>

